Lightningpanda patch 0028: classes that are never released, and a callback object's data in its cell.

Lightningpanda makes each of its classes once per process and every context group (one per
connection, each on its own thread) uses them. A `JSCallbackObject` retained its class when made
and released it when swept, so all those threads wrote one atomic word, the class's reference
count, for every wrapper: eight connections each building and dropping DOM nodes ran 20% slower
than with a set of classes per context group.

`kJSClassAttributeImmortal` (JSForkPrivate.h) on a `JSClassDefinition` says the embedder never
releases the class; its objects then count no reference to it. Releasing such a class while one
of its objects lives is an error.

`JSCallbackObjectData` (the private pointer, the class, the private properties) is a member of
the object instead of an allocation of its own made and freed with every object.

